#: ../../../dev/code_organization.md:3
msgid "Where Code Goes"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../dev/code_organization.md:5
msgid ""
"This page explains how to decide where a piece of code belongs: in an "
"entrypoint, in a helper next to it, in `src/__support/`, or in `shared/`. It "
"also covers which build modes and targets a new function is enabled for. "
"{ref}`source_tree_layout` describes the directories themselves."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../dev/code_organization.md:10
msgid "Three kinds of code"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../dev/code_organization.md:12
msgid "It helps to sort code in a libc into three kinds:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../dev/code_organization.md:14
msgid ""
"**OS independent**: pure computation with no system interaction, such as the "
"math and string functions."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../dev/code_organization.md:16
msgid ""
"**OS agnostic**: standard interfaces whose behavior is the same everywhere "
"but whose implementation needs the system, such as `fopen` or `assert`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../dev/code_organization.md:18
msgid ""
"**OS specific**: interfaces that are specific to a platform. This can be a "
"thin interface like a syscall wrapper or a substantial integration like "
"`popen`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../dev/code_organization.md:22
msgid ""
"The source tree does not separate these three as cleanly as it should, and "
"moving toward that separation is a long-term goal. When adding code, decide "
"which kind it is and keep OS-specific details out of the first two."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../dev/code_organization.md:28
msgid "Entrypoints do not call entrypoints"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../dev/code_organization.md:30
msgid ""
"An entrypoint is the definition of one public function. It may contain its "
"whole implementation. As soon as any part of that implementation is needed "
"somewhere else, that part moves into a separate internal header or library."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../dev/code_organization.md:34
msgid ""
"**An entrypoint must not call another entrypoint.** This is the most common "
"mistake in new code. Calling `strlen` from `strdup`, or `fseek` from "
"`fsetpos`, is the natural way to write the function. It is wrong here "
"because every entrypoint is a separate public symbol in its own separate "
"object file that each target enables or leaves out individually. A "
"dependency between two entrypoints means that they must be pulled in "
"together."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../dev/code_organization.md:41
msgid ""
"Instead, put the shared logic in an internal function and call it from both "
"entrypoints:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../dev/code_organization.md:58
msgid ""
"CMake checks for this. When configured with `-"
"DLIBC_CMAKE_VERBOSE_LOGGING=ON`, it warns about any entrypoint or internal "
"library that depends on an entrypoint. The few permitted exceptions, "
"currently `errno` and `longjmp`, are listed in `check_entrypoint_deps` in "
"`libc/cmake/modules/LLVMLibCObjectRules.cmake`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../dev/code_organization.md:64
msgid ""
"Some existing entrypoints break this rule. They are to be cleaned up and are "
"not precedent for new code."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../dev/code_organization.md:67
msgid ""
"Internal libraries may depend on *variable* entrypoints such as `environ` or "
"`stdout` when they need the object itself. They may not depend on function "
"entrypoints."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../dev/code_organization.md:71
msgid "Where shared logic lives"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../dev/code_organization.md:0
msgid "The logic is used by"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../dev/code_organization.md:0
msgid "Put it in"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../dev/code_organization.md:0
msgid "One entrypoint"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../dev/code_organization.md:0
msgid "The entrypoint's `.cpp` file, as `static` functions."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../dev/code_organization.md:0
msgid "A few entrypoints, normally from the same public header"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../dev/code_organization.md:0
msgid ""
"A header or object library in that header's directory, for example `src/"
"stdio/fflush_internal.h`, in the `internal` namespace."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../dev/code_organization.md:0
msgid "Many parts of the library, or other LLVM projects"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../dev/code_organization.md:0
msgid "`src/__support/`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../dev/code_organization.md:79
msgid ""
"Avoid creating new files or directories named `utils`. A name that describes "
"nothing attracts unrelated code. Name a helper after what it does."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../dev/code_organization.md:82
msgid "Keep internal interfaces as narrow and as typed as public ones:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../dev/code_organization.md:84
msgid ""
"Functions whose result becomes `errno` return `ErrorOr<T>` and never set "
"`errno` themselves. See {ref}`setting_errno`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../dev/code_organization.md:86
msgid ""
"Reuse an existing parser or type before writing a narrower one. For example, "
"`stdio` mode strings are parsed by `FileMode`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../dev/code_organization.md:88
msgid ""
"Write validity checks positively (\"is one of the supported values\"), not "
"as a list of rejected cases."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../dev/code_organization.md:90
msgid ""
"Prefer a fixed signature over an unconstrained template when the body only "
"works for specific types."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../dev/code_organization.md:93
msgid "Code shared with other LLVM projects"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../dev/code_organization.md:95
msgid ""
"Headers in `libc/shared/` expose LLVM-libc internals to other LLVM projects "
"through {ref}`Hand-in-Hand <hand_in_hand>`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../dev/code_organization.md:98
msgid ""
"`shared/` is only an interface. The implementation lives under `libc/src/`, "
"usually in `src/__support/`, and the header in `shared/` re-exports it in "
"the `shared` namespace. Code inside LLVM-libc includes the implementation "
"from `src/` and does not include anything from `shared/`. The GPU RPC "
"headers are an existing exception: they are implemented in `shared/` itself."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../dev/code_organization.md:104
msgid "Everything reachable from `shared/` has extra constraints:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../dev/code_organization.md:106
msgid "It is header-only."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../dev/code_organization.md:107
msgid ""
"It does not depend on any external libc function. The allocator is the one "
"exception, because it is external to LLVM-libc on most targets already."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../dev/code_organization.md:109
msgid ""
"It builds with the compilers LLVM itself supports, which are older than the "
"minimum for the rest of LLVM-libc. See {ref}`compiler_support`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../dev/code_organization.md:112
msgid ""
"This covers the `printf` core, string-to-number conversion, the math "
"implementations in `src/__support/math/`, and what they include from `src/"
"__support/`. Adding a new header to `shared/`, or a new consumer of it, "
"should be discussed with the maintainers first."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../dev/code_organization.md:117
msgid "Operating-system-specific code"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../dev/code_organization.md:119
msgid ""
"Per-OS duplication is acceptable. Operating systems differ enough that "
"sharing an implementation between them usually costs more than it saves."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../dev/code_organization.md:122
msgid ""
"System calls are made only from wrappers in `src/__support/OSUtil/<os>/`; "
"see {ref}`syscall_wrapper_refactor`. OS-agnostic code never names an OS-"
"specific namespace such as `linux_syscalls`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../dev/code_organization.md:126
msgid ""
"There are several ways to select the implementation for the target OS. Pick "
"the one that fits the amount of code that differs:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../dev/code_organization.md:129
msgid ""
"**The whole entrypoint differs.** Put each implementation in `src/<header>/"
"<os>/<func>.cpp`, and make the entrypoint in `src/<header>/CMakeLists.txt` "
"an `ALIAS` that depends on `.${LIBC_TARGET_OS}.<func>`. Most syscall-backed "
"functions work this way."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../dev/code_organization.md:133
msgid ""
"**The entrypoint is common and one step differs.** Keep a single `src/"
"<header>/<func>.cpp` that calls an `internal::` function, and provide that "
"function as a per-OS object library selected with the same `ALIAS` "
"mechanism. `fflush` and its `fflush_internal` library are the example."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../dev/code_organization.md:137
msgid ""
"**A small header-only primitive differs.** Use a dispatch header that "
"includes the right implementation, as `src/__support/OSUtil/io.h` does."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../dev/code_organization.md:140
msgid "Overlay mode and full build"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../dev/code_organization.md:142
msgid ""
"A function may be enabled in {ref}`overlay mode <overlay_mode>` only if it "
"does not depend on an implementation-defined type or on library-owned state "
"that is shared between functions. For example:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../dev/code_organization.md:146
msgid ""
"`strlen` and `round` are pure functions, so they are fine in overlay mode."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../dev/code_organization.md:147
msgid ""
"`fopen` and `fgetc` are full build only, because `FILE` is implementation "
"defined."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../dev/code_organization.md:149
msgid ""
"`atexit` is full build only, because it sets state that `exit` must read."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../dev/code_organization.md:150
msgid ""
"`strtok` is fine in overlay mode. It keeps internal state, but no other "
"function reads that state."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../dev/code_organization.md:153
msgid ""
"In each `entrypoints.txt`, overlay-safe functions are listed unconditionally "
"and the others are inside `if(LLVM_LIBC_FULL_BUILD)`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../dev/code_organization.md:156
msgid ""
"Both modes matter. Overlay mode is the more portable of the two and is how "
"many users adopt the math and memory functions; a full build is where "
"everything else is exercised. A change must build and pass its tests in both "
"modes unless the code is full-build only. See {ref}`testing_guide`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../dev/code_organization.md:161
msgid "Which targets get a new function"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../dev/code_organization.md:163
msgid ""
"A new entrypoint should be enabled on every target where it works. In "
"practice contributors cannot test every target, so the minimum is:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../dev/code_organization.md:166
msgid ""
"For a function that needs Linux, the `entrypoints.txt` files for `x86_64`, "
"`aarch64`, `arm`, and `riscv` under `libc/config/linux/`. Linux on 32-bit "
"Arm is included because its tests help cover the 32-bit Arm code that the "
"baremetal target also uses. See {ref}`platform_support`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../dev/code_organization.md:170
msgid ""
"For an OS-independent function, the Linux configurations above plus the "
"baremetal, GPU, and other OS configurations, as described for math functions "
"in `libc/src/math/docs/add_math_function.md`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../dev/code_organization.md:174
msgid ""
"`linux/x86_64` is the most complete configuration. An entrypoint that is "
"present there and missing from another Linux architecture is usually an "
"oversight, not a decision, and adding it is a welcome change."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../dev/code_organization.md:178
msgid ""
"The public header declaration comes from `libc/include/<header>.yaml`. A "
"function that is missing there will build and pass the tests but will not "
"appear in the generated header."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../dev/code_organization.md:182
msgid "Experimental entrypoints"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../dev/code_organization.md:184
msgid ""
"Entrypoints with known-incomplete implementations are listed inside "
"`if(LLVM_LIBC_ENABLE_EXPERIMENTAL_ENTRYPOINTS)` and are off by default. See "
"{ref}`extensions_and_stubs` for when a function belongs there."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../dev/code_organization.md:188
msgid "Configuration options"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../dev/code_organization.md:190
msgid ""
"Options in `libc/config/config.json` are named `LIBC_CONF_*` and are CMake "
"settings. They become `LIBC_COPT_*` macros on the compiler command line. See "
"{ref}`configure_options`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../dev/code_organization.md:194
msgid ""
"Add an option when a standard requires one behavior and a real user needs "
"another. Disabling floating point support in `printf` for small targets is "
"the model case. Do not add an option to avoid making a design decision."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../dev/code_organization.md:198
msgid ""
"An option that disables behavior in an entrypoint must disable the matching "
"checks in that entrypoint's tests under the same condition."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../dev/code_organization.md:201
msgid "Bazel"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../dev/code_organization.md:203
msgid ""
"The Bazel build in `utils/bazel/llvm-project-overlay/libc/` is maintained by "
"its downstream users, and an automated process keeps it in sync. Upstream "
"contributors are not required to update it, but it is appreciated."
msgstr ""
